Rewrite Break 1 to reflect how CO-RE macros actually work. State that a totally-bogus field name is rejected by clang at COMPILE time — 'error: no member named this_field_does_not_exist in struct task_struct' — because BPF_CORE_READ/bpf_core_field_exists still emit the real C member-access expression (wrapped in __builtin_preserve_access_index / __builtin_preserve_field_info) and clang type-checks it against your vmlinux.h. To exercise the genuine load-time relocation-failure path (libbpf: prog '...': relo #N: failed to relocate ...), you need a field that exists in your build's local BTF but is absent on the TARGET kernel — e.g. compile against a newer vmlinux.h and load on an older kernel that lacks the field. For the graceful-degradation demo, use a field that actually exists so the program compiles, e.g.:

    __u32 fake = 0;
    if (bpf_core_field_exists(task->pid))
        fake = BPF_CORE_READ(task, pid);

and explain that bpf_core_field_exists is for fields that appear/disappear across kernel versions: libbpf evaluates the check at load time using target BTF and patches the branch to a no-op when the field is absent, so one .o runs on kernels that have or lack the field. It is not a way to reference a name that exists in no BTF at all.

